            <ArticleTitle>Elevated concentrations of soluble E-selectin and vascular cell adhesion molecule-1 in NIDDM. Effect of intensive insulin treatment.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1008-13</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the effects of a 14-day intensive insulin therapy and short-term improvement of glycemic control on serum levels of soluble forms of adhesion molecules, i.e., intercellular adhesion molecule-1 (sICAM-1), vascular cell adhesion molecule-1 (sVCAM-1), and E-selectin (sE-selectin) in NIDDM patients with poor glycemic control.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESEARCH DESIGN AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 16 NIDDM patients were compared with 23 healthy subjects (control group) and investigated before and after intensive insulin treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">On day 0, sE-selectin and sVCAM-1 levels were significantly higher in NIDDM patients than in nondiabetic control subjects (median 87, range 63-115; median 544, range 408-797 vs. 58, 43-80; 443, 395-573 ng/ml, respectively) (P &lt; 0.008 in both cases). On day 15, the fall in sE-selectin levels was significant (P &lt; 0.0001) and at a lesser extent in sVCAM-1 levels (64, 48-85; 506, 417-678 ng/ml, respectively); these levels reached values that no longer differed from those of control subjects (P = 0.23 and 0.15, respectively). Moreover, the fall in sE-selectin was positively associated with the change in LDL cholesterol and the improvement of glycemia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In poorly controlled NIDDM patients, sE-selectin levels are increased and significantly fall to normal after short-term improvement of glycemic control. This suggests that assaying sE-selectin makes it possible to detect endothelium activation and to follow its reversal with euglycemia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
